\value{
\code{go_*()} functions return a graph object;
  \code{add_*()} functions return the modified figure.
}
\description{
The data the describes the coordinates of the surface is set in `z`. Data
in `z` should be a matrix. Coordinates in `x` and `y` can either be 1D
vectors or matrices (e.g. to graph parametric surfaces). If not provided
in `x` and `y`, the x and y coordinates are assumed to be linear starting
at 0 with a unit step. The color scale corresponds to the `z` values by
default. For custom color scales, use `surfacecolor` which should be a
matrix, where its bounds can be controlled using `cmin` and `cmax`.
}
\details{
\code{go_surface()} creates a validated graph object (a named list).
\code{add_surface()} creates one and adds it to a figure.


Nested objects are given as named lists or with their constructors, which
are linked from the arguments below. Every property is checked against
the plotly.js schema when the object is created or modified (see
\code{\link{plotly_obj-modify}}). One-sided formulas (\code{~col}) are
evaluated in the data, and then checked, when the trace is added with
\code{add_<trace>()} or \code{add_trace()}.
}
\examples{
go_figure() |>
  add_surface(z = volcano)
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{go_figure}()}, \code{\link{add_trace}()}
}
